#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221132 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "4874a486-2cf1-5b6e-ae4f-52579f03b077" :question/math-academy-id "e-21695" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2$, shown above. Find the fundamental matrix for the system." :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $\\Phi(t)$ of the system is an $n \\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2 \\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nx_{2}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}e^{5t} = \\begin{bmatrix}4te^{5t} \\\\ 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{5t} & 4te^{5t} \\\\ 0 & 4e^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi$ can be written in any order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/id #uuid "3e053d6d-f051-5c4c-a20b-1e90b2d5941b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21696" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for this system." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\mathbf x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t)\n$$\n\ndenoted $\\Phi_0(t)$, is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\quad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the identity matrix.\n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{At} &= α_{1}At + α_{0}I \\\\\n&= α_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t + α_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}α_{0} & -7α_{1}t \\\\ 7α_{1}t & α_{0}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial\n\n$$\nr(\\lambda) = \\alpha_1 \\lambda + \\alpha_0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $At$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 7\\textrm{i}t, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = -7\\textrm{i}t\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr((7\\textrm{i})t) &= e^{(7\\textrm{i})t} \\\\\nα_{1}(7\\textrm{i})t + α_{0} &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t) \\\\\nα_{0} + 7\\textrm{i}α_{1}t &= \\cos (7t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0{:}$\n\n$$\n{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); 7α_{1}t = \\sin (7t)⇒{α_{0} = \\cos (7t); α_{1} = \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\alpha_1$ and $\\alpha_0$ into the expressions for the required entries of $e^{At}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nα_{0} &= \\cos (7t) \\\\\n7α_{1}t &= 7 \\cdot \\frac{1}{7t}\\sin (7t) \\cdot t \\\\\n&= \\sin (7t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (7t) & -\\sin (7t) \\\\ \\sin (7t) & \\cos (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Properties of a Fundamental Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "a52a6539-60d1-557c-a042-cbce5616e94f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21697" :question/problem "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[\\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.*\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}$. Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)$. This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n$\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}$. We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n$\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big] = A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$.\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}$. From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$. Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1}$, we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t)$. Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Fundamental Matrices Using Matrix Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "865bf3a6-76e8-5869-98db-5513a7a2eb59" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220507} {:answer-field/id #uuid "eae83b61-2333-54f1-9d36-26dcb5ecd3cd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220508} {:answer-field/id #uuid "6fd342b8-b384-552b-a748-4e8f94c43afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220509} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7e89df62-c8be-5815-b2fb-bcb692f5d2e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220510}] :question/id #uuid "5ca7cf8a-29b9-5dc9-9eb7-2eb98441be57" :question/math-academy-id "q-333713" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $x^{′} = Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| $Φ_{0}(t) =$